430: 
431: | x-value | profile |
432: |---|---|
433: | 0 | 3,3,3,3 |
434: | 1 | 4,4,4 |
435: | infinity | 12 |
436: 
437: Indeed near b=0 the four roots of a⁴=-1 are distinct, so b is a local
438: parameter and x=b³. Near a=0 there are three points and x-1=a⁴. The
439: coprimality of 3 and4 gives one point at infinity, with poles of orders3
440: and4 for a,b respectively, and hence pole order12 for x. The total
441: ramification is8+9+11=28, and Riemann--Hurwitz gives 2g-2=-24+28=4.
442: 
443: For the E26 base with IV*,III,I1 over0,1,infinity, the pullback by t=x
444: has a single I12 fibre after minimalization, by local semistable base
445: change of the nodal model. There is a different map on the very same
446: curve that gives twelve I1 fibres. Choose a regular finite value t*
447: different from0,1 and set
448: 
449:     t=mu(x)=t* x/(x+t*-1).
450: 
451: This sends the three branch values0,1,infinity to0,1,t*. Its inverse
452: image of the source cusp infinity is the regular x-value1-t*, and so
453: has12 distinct points, all unramified. The pullback has twelve I1
454: fibres. Its order3 and order4 branches still remove IV* and III, while
455: the order12 branch lies over a smooth elliptic fibre. The curve alone
456: therefore does not specify which of these fibrations is being used.
457: The Milnor page of a regular (3,4) Seifert fibre uses a regular binding
458: value t* and leads to the second map. The actual four-dimensional
459: page, its boundary surgeries and its real trace are constructed in
460: P3K.6--7.
461: 
462: In either compact minimal pullback, the canonical formula gives
463: K=5F, chi(O)=1, and sigma=-8. The height pulls back to2. All multiples
464: of P meet the identity component in the I12 case, because P already
465: meets the smooth locus of the original I1 fibre; there are no reducible
466: fibres in the twelve-I1 case. Hence (mP).O=m²-1 in both cases.
467: 
468: The parity test in P3F.5 only excludes the normal Euler class2M for a
469: ball-puncture of this nonspin compact surface. It does not exclude the
470: square required in the prompt: e=K+2M is characteristic, and
471: e²=4M² because K²=K.M=0. No conclusion about the actual relative normal
472: class follows until its cap and framing have been identified.
473: 
474: ## P3F.7. Proved: the orbit pair and the local conifold surgery
475: 
476: For the AC26 sphere member, its delta circle is semifree, has one fixed
477: curve F=P1, and its orbit pair is diffeomorphic to the standard pair
478: (S5,S2). Its fixed normal bundle is O(-1) plus O(-1) with respective
479: weights(-1,+1). Replacing that fixed neighbourhood by the conifold
480: smoothing gives a free circle manifold S3 times S3, with quotient
481: S2 times S3.
482: 
483: Proof. On an ordinary fibre the delta circle is primitive and acts
484: freely. At a finite cap, a nontrivial deck power has nonintegral gamma
485: character, since gamma(v_j) is prime to m_j. It therefore cannot agree
486: with a delta circle rotation. This proves freeness there. In the cusp
487: fan the directions are ±e1,±e2,±(e1-e2). A finite circle stabilizer at
488: an edge of direction d has order dividing det(d,e2), which is±1 off
489: the e2 edges; on an e2 edge the entire circle fixes it. At the two-dimensional torus orbit corresponding to a vertex of the
490: height-one triangulation there is no stabilizer. The zero-dimensional
491: torus orbits corresponding to triangles are fixed, and each lies on
492: one of the fixed edges. The period lattice acts transitively on the e2 edges, with no
493: stabilizer, so their quotient is one fixed P1.
494: 
495: For the vertical wall use the four rays
496: 
497:     r0=(0,0,1), r1=(0,1,1), r+=(1,0,1), r-=(-1,1,1).
498: 
499: They satisfy r++r-=r0+r1. The two adjacent unimodular charts have
500: coordinates(x0,x1,z) and(x0',x1',z'), with exact transition
501: 
502:     x0'=z x0,  x1'=z x1,  z'=1/z,
503:     q=x0 x1 z=x0' x1' z'.
504: 
505: The fixed curve is x0=x1=0. The transitions give O(-1)², and the
506: cocharacter e2 has weights(-1,+1,0). These identities are independently
507: checked from the ray matrices in code/part3/orbit_mv.py. The map
508: 
509:     A=x0, B=z x0, C=x1, D=z x1
510: 
511: contracts the curve to AD=BC. It intertwines the circle with weights
512: (-1,-1,+1,+1) on(A,B,C,D). On AD-BC=epsilon nonzero the action is free.
513: For epsilon=1, polar decomposition of SL2(C) as U H gives the explicit
514: equivariant diffeomorphism SU2 times R3, with the circle acting on U
515: by left multiplication and acting trivially on H. A compact Milnor
516: neighbourhood is thus S3 times D3 and its quotient is S2 times D3.
517: 
518: For completeness, the orbit is identified integrally. Let A=X minus F
519: and B=A/S1. General position gives pi1(A)=0. Alexander duality gives
520: H*(A;Z)=Z in degrees0,3 and zero otherwise. The oriented normal link
521: S3 is a primitive generator of H3(A). Its quotient is the normal link
522: S2, and the circle bundle on these links is the Hopf bundle. In the
523: Gysin sequence, e generates H2(B), and integration H3(A)->H2(B) is
524: multiplication by1: evaluate on this linked S2 and its Hopf preimage.
525: Every other positive-degree group of B vanishes. Also pi1(B)=0 by the
526: homotopy sequence of the principal circle bundle.
527: 
528: Conjugating the negative-weight line converts the fixed normal bundle
529: to a positive-weight complex bundle O(1) plus O(-1). Its clutching loop
530: diag(z,z^-1) in SU2 bounds the explicit disc with SU2 coordinates
531: (a,b)=(r exp(i theta),sqrt(1-r²)); joining its centre to the identity
532: gives a nullhomotopy. Thus it is a trivial unitary rank2 bundle. Its
533: orbit normal bundle is a trivial oriented rank3 bundle. With this
534: trivialization the boundary is F times S2, and the Euler class is just
535: the meridian generator, with no F term.
536: 
537: Now Y=X/S1 is the union of B (truncated at that boundary) and F times
538: D3. The maps on H0 and H2 in Mayer--Vietoris, with domain H2 ordered
539: (F,meridian) and target(complement,tube), are respectively
540: 
541:     [1;-1],       [[0,1],[-1,0]].
542: 
543: The latter has determinant1. H4(boundary)=Z maps to zero and gives
544: H5(Y)=Z; every other positive intermediate group is zero. All matrices
545: and their Smith forms are printed in orbit_mv.out. Van Kampen gives
546: pi1(Y)=0. Thus Y is a homotopy5-sphere. Kervaire--Milnor1963, the table
547: on p505 and the Smale comparison stated immediately above it, gives
548: Theta5=0, so Y is diffeomorphic to S5. Haefliger1966 Corollary6.6 says
549: C_n^q=0 for n<2q-3; with n=2,q=3, and Theorem1.2 converting concordance
550: to isotopy, every embedding S2 in S5 is unknotted. This identifies the
551: orbit pair.
552: 
553: One may now construct the equivariant identification with the standard
554: circle action on the sphere boundary of D3 times D4. On the free
555: complement the principal circle bundle over S2 times D3 has Euler
556: generator and is the product Hopf bundle S3 times D3. On the fixed
557: tube it is the trivial positive-weight rank2 bundle just constructed.
558: Their two boundary identifications differ by a gauge map from S2 times
559: S2 to S1. Such a map has a global logarithm because H1(S2 times S2;Z)=0,
560: so it extends over a collar and removes the discrepancy. This gives an
561: equivariant diffeomorphism. It is also the specialization of Levine's
562: classification as stated in Harvey--Kerin--Shankar2020 Theorem4.1,
563: whose hypotheses are checked here: smooth codimension4 fixed set and
564: H2(F;Z) without2-torsion. Replacing the fixed tube S2 times D4 by the
565: free piece D3 times S3 doubles the free complement, giving S3 timesS3;
566: the quotient doubles S2 timesD3, giving S2 timesS3. The normal framing
567: is unique up to homotopy because pi2(SO4)=0. This is the asserted
568: conifold surgery. End of proof.
569: 
570: ## P3F.8. Proved: the actual equivariant family has a typed Hopf-gluing image
571: 
572: Let Phi be the actual positive parameter return map of the sphere
573: family, with the complex orientation, represented by an equivariant
574: Ehresmann trivialization. There is a Torelli diffeomorphism f of
575: S2 timesS3, supported in S2 timesD3, for which the oriented sphere
576: clutched by Phi is exactly the Hopf-lift gluing lambda(f) of P3F.4.
577: This statement identifies the morphism; it does not assume values of
578: the two integer invariants of f.
579: 
580: Proof. The parameter family carries the delta circle action. Its fixed
581: curves form a P1 bundle around the parameter circle; the two normal
582: weight lines have degree-1. The fixed-curve bundle is smoothly trivial
583: because PGL2(C) is connected. Each weight line over P1 timesS1 is
584: isomorphic as a smooth complex line bundle to the pullback of O(-1),
585: since H2(P1 timesS1;Z)=Z. Thus its equivariant normal disc bundle can
586: be trivialized around the parameter circle. Choose an Ehresmann
587: connection agreeing with that trivialization near the fixed set;
588: averaging over the compact circle makes it equivariant. Its return map
589: is the identity near the fixed tube.
590: 
591: Under P3F.7 write the sphere as
592: 
593:     (S2_y timesD4_u) union (D3_y timesS3_u),
594: 
595: with the circle the Hopf action on the u coordinate. Conjugating the negative
596: normal line reverses its real two-plane orientation; choose the
597: orientation of the y factor so that this identification preserves the
598: complex orientation of X. The restriction
599: of Phi to the free part is an equivariant map tilde f of D3_y timesS3_u
600: equal to the identity near its boundary. Its quotient is a map of
601: D3_y timesS2_u relative boundary. Extend it by the identity over the
602: other hemisphere of S3_y. This defines f:S3_y timesS2_u -> S3_y timesS2_u.
603: It fixes the H2 generator, since that generator is represented in the
604: identity hemisphere. It preserves orientation, so Poincare duality
605: makes it fix H3 as well. Consequently f is in the Torelli group.
606: 
607: Its Hopf lift A on S3_y timesS3_u equals tilde f on the first
608: hemisphere and the identity on the second. Any other lift differs by
609: a gauge map S2 timesS3 -> S1. Its H1 is zero, so that map has a global
610: logarithm and yields an isotopy of lifts.
611: 
612: Here is the cut-and-reglue comparison. In the standard sphere
613: boundary(D4_u timesD4_y), put
614: 
615:     H=D4_u timesS3_y,  K=S3_u timesD4_y,
616:     N=S3_u timesD3_y in boundary H=boundary K.
617: 
618: Our gluing convention identifies x in boundary H with A(x) in
619: boundary K, after the standard product-boundary identification.
620: The ball B7=D4_u timesD3_y has boundary equal to N union
621: (D4_u timesS2_y). Its complement in the rounded product boundary is
622: the other standard7-ball: this follows by taking the two hemispheres
623: of the y boundary and rounding their product collars. Regluing H to
624: K by A changes the identification only on N. Equivalently cut out B7
625: and reglue its boundary by the map equal to tilde f on N and the
626: identity on D4_u timesS2_y. That boundary map is precisely Phi in the
627: displayed decomposition of S6. The identity on the common collar
628: makes the two quotient constructions coincide pointwise outside N,
629: and their collar identifications coincide on N. Hence
630: 
631:     D7 union_Phi D7  =  (D4_u timesS3_y) union_A (S3_u timesD4_y)
632: 
633: as oriented smooth manifolds, with outward boundary orientation on
634: the first piece and the complex sphere orientation used to choose
635: the initial identification. This is lambda(f). End of proof.
636: 
637: The actual sixfold descent of P2.1 is
638: 
639:     H(t)delta, H(t)=-(4l1 log t+3l2 log(t-1))/(2pi i),
640: 
641: with cusp coefficient -p after carrying the collar integer l0.
642: P3K.6--7 constructs its real trace with the complete line clutch.
643: P3K.9 evaluates its characteristic pair as(-6,-24), including the
644: normal-frame comparison, and descends it to(-1,-4) for one parameter
645: loop. The forward geometric mapping-torus orientation and Jin's
646: reverse convention are both included in P3K.7.
647: 
648: ## P3F.9. The resolved cusp orbit as a nodal elliptic neighbourhood times a circle
649: 
650: The local calculation giving the product is as follows. Start with the
651: ordinary conifold xy=uv, with the circle acting on u,v with weights+1,-1
652: and fixing x,y. A small resolution has charts
653: 
654:     (x,v,z),   u=xz, y=vz;
655:     (u,y,w),   x=uw, v=yw;      w=1/z.
656: 
657: The orbit charts, with the Hopf smoothing defined using these normal
658: coordinates, are
659: 
660:     (x,y,b), b=(|z|²-|v|²)/2;
661:     (x,y,b'), b'=(|u|²-|w|²)/2.
662: 
663: On the overlap r=|z|²>0 they satisfy
664: 
665:     2b=r-|y|²/r,       2b'=|x|²r-1/r,
666:     db'/db=(|x|²+r^(-2))/(1+|y|²r^(-2))>0.       (P3F.9.1)
667: 
668: Thus projection to(x,y) is a smooth submersion with oriented
669: one-dimensional fibres. Every fibre is a line: away from(x,y)=(0,0)
670: the two charts join its negative and positive ends in the displayed
671: increasing order; over(0,0) the exceptional interval joins the two
672: half-lines. A smooth section can be chosen in the free part. For
673: epsilon>0 choose the unique r>0 solving
674: 
675:     |x|²r-|y|²/r+2epsilon(r-1)/(r+1)=0.
676: 
677: The left side increases strictly from a negative to a positive value;
678: its derivative is |x|²+|y|²/r²+4epsilon/(r+1)²>0. Consequently r is a
679: smooth positive function of(x,y), and the representatives
680: z=sqrt(r),v=y/sqrt(r),u=x sqrt(r) give the section in the orbit.
681: A positive vertical vector field, patched by a partition of unity and
682: rescaled to be complete, identifies each line with R by flow from this
683: section. It therefore gives a product diffeomorphism over C².
684: 
685: This comparison can be made relative to a prescribed product
686: identification outside the node neighbourhood. The quotient smoothing
687: uses a choice of invariant metric in the fixed normal bundle; choose
688: it to agree with the original product normal metric outside that
689: neighbourhood. There projection to(x,y) is the original smooth
690: submersion. On overlapping local line trivialisations the transitions
691: are increasing by(P3F.9.1). Taking a partition of unity on the base and
692: a convex combination of increasing, proper fibre coordinates produces
693: an increasing proper coordinate agreeing with the prescribed one where
694: the outer partition has weight1. This is a relative product
695: trivialization. Its derivative over the fixed (x,y) base is an upper
696: triangular real matrix with identity base block and a positive fibre
697: derivative. Contracting the off-diagonal block and then the positive
698: scalar gives a homotopy to the identity relative to the prescribed
699: outer product. Thus this comparison retains the stable boundary
700: framing, not just the orbit diffeomorphism type. In particular one must not equate all quadratic
701: invariants in different normal metrics at a fixed point: their
702: transition can involve a radial square root. The metric choice and the
703: positive-coordinate construction avoid that error.
704: 
705: Apply this calculation to the square Mumford fan before triangulation.
706: The square model is the fibre product of the two nodal elliptic models;
707: the A2 triangulation is its small resolution, one per lattice cell.
708: The circle acts in the second nodal factor. Its orbit is a smooth
709: circle bundle over the base disc, since its local quotient at the node
710: is(q=uv,(|u|²-|v|²)/2). It is a product over the disc. Before resolving
711: the conifold, the total orbit is therefore E_I1 timesS1, where E_I1 is
712: a compact neighbourhood of one nodal elliptic fibre in a smooth
713: elliptic surface. The relative comparison above replaces the node
714: orbit without changing this product diffeomorphism type or its outer
715: boundary marking.
716: 
717: The bounded toric units of the actual construction do not change this
718: statement. Choose their logarithms on the cusp disc. Their interpolation
719: to constant units gives the same unimodular charts and a proper smooth
720: family after shrinking the disc uniformly; the logarithmic terms
721: dominate every bounded unit. Averaging an Ehresmann field gives an
722: equivariant comparison. In the source elliptic factor, the gamma deck
723: translates by the section6P. Its Tate coordinate is a unit a(q), and
724: the explicit path Z->exp(t log a(q)) Z extends across the nodal fibre.
725: Its suspension is consequently a product E_I1 timesS1_gamma. The
726: source Picard line restricted to an elliptic neighbourhood selected
727: disjoint from the fixed sphere is topologically trivial: its degree on
728: the nodal fibre is zero, and that neighbourhood retracts onto the nodal
729: fibre. An extending Hermitian metric removes its radial unit on this
730: slice. This statement does not trivialize the principal-circle line on
731: the complement of the fixed sphere in the entire orbit neighbourhood.
732: That line has Chern number one, up to the fixed orientation convention,
733: on a meridian two-sphere; its meridian class is retained in every
734: subsequent clutch or index comparison. These comparisons preserve the integral
735: gamma,u,w and q markings on the outer boundary. Changes in the affine
736: stable frames consist of unit scalings and triangular shears and are
737: homotopic to the indicated diagonal factors; no three-dimensional
738: Pontryagin framing class is introduced by a logarithm of a unit.
739: 
740: The fixed sphere projects in E_I1 to the normalization of the nodal
741: fibre. The small-resolution interval separates its two preimages of
742: the node in the gamma direction. Its projected normal complex line
743: has transition a'=z²a, namely O(-2). Its Euler number, with the complex
744: orientation of that line and of the normalization, is-2. The gamma
745: coordinate of this sphere can be confined to an arbitrarily short arc
746: by taking the relative node comparison sufficiently small. Rotation of
747: the S1_gamma factor is a free action on the cusp orbit body and extends
748: the marked boundary gamma circle action.
749: 
750: This proves the claimed product comparison with the explicit boundary
